<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayer</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayer is a German chemical and pharmaceutical company founded in the nineteenth century. Along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basf">BASF</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoechst">Hoechst</a>, it led the chemical industry of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, which by 1913 dominated the global market for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">synthetic dyes</a>. The company is an example of the science-based industrial growth that made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> the largest economy in continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayer.jpg" alt="Bayer" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bayer AG · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_Bayer.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bayer was founded in 1863 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmen">Barmen</a>, later part of Wuppertal, as a partnership between the dye salesman Friedrich Bayer and the master dyer Johann Friedrich Weskott. Bayer handled the commercial side. The company began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">dyestuffs</a> producer, and fuchsine and aniline became its most important products. In 1866 its headquarters and most production moved to a larger site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elberfeld">Elberfeld</a>. Friedrich Bayer's son, also named Friedrich Bayer, a chemist, joined it in 1873. After the founder's death in 1880 the firm became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joint-stock-company">joint-stock company</a>, Farbenfabriken vorm. Friedr. Bayer &amp; Co, also known as the Elberfelder Farbenfabriken. The versatility of aniline chemistry led it to expand into other areas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bayer's first major product was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspirin">acetylsalicylic acid</a>, first described by the French chemist Charles Frederic Gerhardt in 1853, a modification of salicylic acid, found in willow bark, which was a folk remedy. By 1899 the trademark Aspirin was registered worldwide. From 1898 to 1910 Bayer also trademarked and marketed heroin, introduced as a non-addictive substitute for morphine, as a cough suppressant and remedy for ailments including pneumonia and tuberculosis. Its research led to the discovery of phenobarbital in 1911 and of its anti-epileptic activity in 1912. The Bayer Cross trademark of 1904 was stamped onto every aspirin tablet from 1910.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, Bayer's assets and trademarks were confiscated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and several other countries, and in the United States and Canada they passed to Sterling Drug and were not reclaimed until 1994. Bayer also developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a>, and in 1914 it made a lung irritant for 105 mm artillery shells. In 1925 it merged with five other German companies, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basf">BASF</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoechst">Hoechst</a>, into IG Farben. Gerhard Domagk, working with the chemists Fritz Mietzsch and Joseph Klarer, discovered prontosil, the first commercially available antibacterial drug, in the 1930s and won the 1939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> forced him to relinquish.</p><p>IG Farben used slave labour in factories it built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-concentration-camps">Nazi concentration camps</a>, most notably at Monowitz, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> complex. By 1943 almost half of its 330,000 workers were slave labourers or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, including 30,000 Auschwitz prisoners. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Allied Control Council seized its assets, and in 1951 it was split into its constituent companies, Bayer being reincorporated as Farbenfabriken Bayer AG.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Bayer did much to bring about the postwar West German economic recovery and regained its position as one of the world's largest chemical and pharmaceutical companies. It re-hired several former Nazis to high positions, among them Fritz ter Meer, who had been convicted at the IG Farben Trial. The company is headquartered in Leverkusen, and in 2016 it merged with Monsanto.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1913 German chemical firms such as BASF, <b>Bayer</b> and Hoechst supplied roughly 80 to 90 per cent of the world's synthetic dyes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
